t` against this repo |\n22-\n23:**The `url` field is the material improvement over Q38.** Q38 recovered slug + `scriptVersionId` for the\n24-teammate submissions by a separate route and left our own side implicit. The submission object carries both\n25-directly, for every submission, so all seven candidates are now **known** rather than inferred.\n26-\n27-## 2. The inventory\n28-\n29:| ref | public | account | kernel slug | scriptVersionId | repo package | datasets | git commit | output archived |\n30-|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n31-| `54922806` | **6.563** | leemarc223 | `leemarc223/rogii-public-pf-frontier-rerun-20260723` | **337355891** | `reports/teammate_kernels/rogii-public-pf-frontier-rerun-20260723` | 7 | **MISSING** | **NO — §4** |\n32-| `54968060` | 6.643 | joezzzzz | `joezzzzz/rogii-kaiwalya-overlap-off-full` | 337787958 | `kaggle_kernel_kaiwalya_overlap_off_full` | 9 | `9857c3a` ✔in repo | **yes, verified** |\n33-| `54844628` | 7.891 | joezzzzz | `joezzzzz/rogii-struct-field-blend-codex` | 336599680 | `kaggle_kernel_struct_field_blend` | 1 | `d972986` ✔in repo | **yes, verified** |\n34-| `55064411` | 6.695 | joezzzzz | `joezzzzz/rogii-frontier-hedgeoff-full` | 338650633 | `kaggle_kernel_frontier_hedgeoff_full` | 9 | `f491826` ✔in repo | **yes, verified** |\n35-| `54990075` | 6.690 | joezzzzz | `joezzzzz/rogii-frontier-sp45-only-full` | 337982098 | `kaggle_kernel_frontier_sp45only_full` | 9 | `dd4e79e` ✔in repo | **yes, verified** |\n36-| `54896975` | 6.669 | leemarc223 | `leemarc223/rogii-kaiwalya-public-tvt-6-626-repro` | 337097140 | `reports/teammate_kernels/rogii-kaiwalya-public-tvt-6-626-repro` (+ `origin/main` `a589fa8`) | 9 | **MISSING** | **yes, verified** |\n37-| `54923144` | 6.678 | leemarc223 | `leemarc223/rogii-gr-sigma-1-0-frontier-20260723` | 337362917 | `reports/teammate_kernels/rogii-gr-sigma-1-0-frontier-20260723` | 7 | **MISSING** | **NO — §4** |\n38-\n39:All seven are on team **`lee Marc223`**, `submitted_by` split **4 joezzzzz / 3 leemarc223**.\n40-\n41-### Field status (task step 3)\n42-\n43-| field | known | inferred | missing | inaccessible |\n44-|---|---|---|---|---|\n45-| ref, score, date, account, team | **7/7** | — | — | — |\n46-| kernel slug | **7/7** | — | — | — |\n47:| scriptVersionId | **7/7** | — | — | — |\n48-| code in repo | **7/7** (4 own packages, 3 pulled teammate packages) | — | — | — |\n49:| dataset_sources | **7/7 for the CURRENT kernel version** | 7/7 for the *submitted* version — see the limit in §5 | — | — |\n50-| git commit | 4/7 | — | **3/7** — the teammate submissions carry no commit id, and this repo is not where they were run | — |\n51:| output submission file | **5/7 verified** | — | **2/7** (`54922806`, `54923144`) | the *submitted-version* output is not reachable by API — §4 |\n52-\n53-## 3. The outputs are archived and independently verified\n54-\n55:`api.kernels_output()` was used to retrieve each candidate's output; every file was checked against\n56-`data/rogii/sample_submission.csv` — **14,151 rows, id set equal, id order equal, all finite** for all seven.\n57-Five are archived in `reports/submission_outputs/` with a provenance README.\n58-\n59-Verification did not stop at \"the file parses\". Each archived file is tied to its submission by a number\n60-recorded **at submission time**, independently of this round:\n61-\n62-```\n63-pairwise RMSE between archived outputs (ft)\n64-             54844628  54896975  54968060  54990075  55064411\n65-54844628        0.000     4.020     2.537     2.601     1.878\n66-54896975        4.020     0.000     3.105     2.989     2.582\n67-54968060        2.537     3.105     0.000     1.640     1.756\n68-54990075        2.601     2.989     1.640     0.000     1.457\n69-55064411        1.878     2.582     1.756     1.457     0.000\n70-```\n71-\n72-- `54990075`'s description records *\"rmse 1.640 vs 54968060\"* → measured **1.640** ✔\n73-- `55064411`'s description records *\"rmse 1.756 vs 54968060\"* → measured **1.756** ✔\n74-- `54968060`'s description records *\"rmse 3.1\"* vs the overlap-ON family and *\"rmse 2.54\"* vs `54844628`\n75-  → measured **3.105** and **2.537** ✔\n76-- `54896975`'s description records *\"SHA b192d3\"* → archived sha256 is **`b192d3f348ae…`** ✔\n77-\n78:So five archived artifacts are confirmed to be the submitted ones, by evidence written before this round\n79-existed.\n80-\n81:## 4. The finding — `54922806`'s submitted output is not retrievable, and its kernel has moved\n82-\n83-`54922806`, `54896975` and `54923144` scored **6.563 / 6.669 / 6.678**. Their kernels' currently-retrievable\n84:outputs are **byte-identical to each other**:\n85-\n86-```\n87-b192d3f348ae00680dc4df942b95cef5fd708c636a741f77dfb6b6e89b9ded4a   54896975  (matches its recorded SHA)\n88:b192d3f348ae00680dc4df942b95cef5fd708c636a741f77dfb6b6e89b9ded4a   54922806  <- cannot be its artifact\n89:b192d3f348ae00680dc4df942b95cef5fd708c636a741f77dfb6b6e89b9ded4a   54923144  <- cannot be its artifact\n90-```\n91-\n92-Three different public scores cannot come from one prediction file. The SHA match identifies the shared file\n93-as **`54896975`'s**, so the copies retrieved under the other two refs are **post-submission re-runs**, not\n94:submitted artifacts. They were deleted rather than archived, because a file named `54922806_submission.csv`\n95-that is not `54922806`'s output is worse than no file at all.\n96-\n97-**This answers, with evidence, the question Q38 left open.** Q38's message to the teammate asked: *\"Have any\n98-of these kernels been edited or re-run after the submissions below?\"* The three kernels now converging on one\n99-output is direct evidence that at least two of them have been. It does not require a reply to establish.\n100-\n101-**Why it matters beyond bookkeeping.** `54922806` is the **score-first slot-1 candidate**. Under Winner's\n102:Obligations the submitted artifact and the code that produced it must be disclosable, and right now:\n103-\n104-- the *code* is archived (Q38 pulled it; Q39 diffed it — pristine base + `*1.3` on the GR sigma at cell 29);\n105:- the *submitted output* is not, and `api.kernels_output` cannot return it because it serves only the latest\n106-  version;\n107:- the specific `scriptVersionId` **337355891** is known, so the artifact is not lost — it is one UI action\n108-  away, but that action is the owner's.\n109-\n110-This does **not** change the slot recommendation. Q44 already found the two slot-1 options tied on\n111-measurement, with ownership as the tie-breaker; this adds one more, independent, ownership-flavoured\n112-consideration on the same side, and it is recorded as such rather than presented as decisive.\n113-\n114-## 5. Limits\n115-\n116:- **`dataset_sources` is read from the current kernel package, not from the submitted version.** Kaggle's API\n117:  exposes metadata for the latest version only. Given §4 shows at least two teammate kernels have moved since\n118:  submission, the dataset list for *those* submitted versions is inferred, not known. For the four `joezzzzz`\n119:  kernels the archived output was verified as the submitted artifact (§3), which makes the current package a\n120-  much safer proxy — but it is still a proxy.\n121-- The three teammate submissions carry **no git commit**, and there is no reason they should: they were not\n122-  run from this repo. The repo package is the substitute, and for `54896975` `origin/main` `a589fa8` also\n123-  holds it.\n124-- `54853492` is excluded by standing instruction. `54777533` is noted only because Q38 recorded its kernel\n125-  belongs to a **third-party account** (`qwer556617123/rogii-prvs-oof-meta-direct-fast`) — a disclosure item\n126-  if it were ever selected, which it is not.\n127:- The archive is 2.3 MB for 5 files; it is committed deliberately, since the whole point is that the artifact\n128-  exists locally and is auditable without a network call.\n129-- `total_bytes` on a submission object (~31 MB) is the **output bundle**, not the CSV (~464 KB). Recorded so\n130-  a future round does not read a size mismatch as corruption.\n131-\n132-## 6. Owner actions required (task step 4)\n133-\n134-Ordered by how much they matter for the final slots. None can be done by an agent — each needs the owner's\n135-Kaggle session or the teammate.\n136-\n137:1. **Retrieve `54922806`'s submitted output** — Kaggle → `leemarc223/rogii-public-pf-frontier-rerun-20260723`\n138:   → Version history → **scriptVersionId 337355891** → Output → download `submission.csv`, and drop it in\n139-   `reports/submission_outputs/`. This is the only missing asset attached to a **recommended slot-1\n140:   candidate**. *(If `54968060` is chosen for slot 1 instead, this becomes optional — its artifact is already\n141-   archived and verified.)*\n142-2. **Ask the teammate to confirm the post-submission state of the three kernels** — Q38's question, now with\n143-   §4's evidence attached: three kernels that scored differently currently emit one identical file. Worth\n144-   asking specifically whether `rogii-public-pf-frontier-rerun-20260723` still contains the `*1.3` GR-sigma\n145-   token that Q39 measured in its pulled source.\n146:3. **Do nothing for `54923144`** unless it becomes a slot candidate. Its artifact has the same gap but it is\n147-   not recommended under any of the four selection views.\n148-4. **Winner's-obligation disclosure lists are already prepared** in Q30 §6 and unchanged by this round:\n149:   `54844628` needs `ravaghi/wellbore-geology-prediction-artifacts`; the frontier candidates need all 9\n150-   attached datasets plus the derivation from the public notebook `kaiwalyaatulraut/rogii-public-tvt-solution`;\n151-   `54922806` needs those plus the teammate-supplied kernel version — **which item 1 above now supplies**\n152:   (scriptVersionId 337355891, recorded here).\n153-5. **One provenance item remains unverified from Q30 §7 and is not closed by this round:**\n154:   `ravaghi/wellbore-geology-prediction-artifacts` ships a `data/train.csv` described in our own kernel as a\n155-   cache. It is required by **both** slots, so it is a team-level disclosure item, not a slot-selection input.\n156-\n157-## 7. Next\n158-\n159-`q47_final_selection_ui_checklist` is next by priority. `q58_honest_kernel_backward_smoothing` has its NS=64\n160-producer in flight (ETA ~02:50 UTC) and will be collected when it lands.\n161-`q29_final_slot_candidate_packager` reactivates **2026-08-03**; the final selection is due **2026-08-04** and\n162-costs no quota.\n1:# Q58 — carrying the PF smoother to the deployed honest line: the stage is now exactly reproduced, and the end-to-end run is in flight\n2-\n3-Date: 2026-07-29 23:50 UTC\n4-Task: `q58_honest_kernel_backward_smoothing` (`requires_gpu=false`, `can_submit=true`, `max_submit_cost=1`)\n5-Scripts: `scripts/q58_smoothed_pf_produce.py` (producer), `scripts/q58_struct_refit.py` (evaluator)\n6-Logs: `reports/logs/q58_pf_produce_2026-07-30.log`\n7:Outcome: **the deployed honest line is now reproduced exactly from saved arrays (§2); the NS=64 row-level PF\n8-run is in flight, ETA ~02:50 UTC. No submission yet — the decision needs the end-to-end number. Quota 0/5.**\n9-\n10-## 1. The structural-field stage does not depend on the PF — which changes what \"re-fit\" means\n11-\n12-The task's premise was that the structural-field stage \"was fitted against the forward PF\" and might absorb,\n13-keep or destroy the smoother's gain. Reading `scripts/struct_oof_produce.py` rather than assuming, the stage\n14-is:\n15-\n16-```\n17-group   = round(max(typewell.TVT), 1)                 # the target's OWN typewell -> test-available\n--\n27-```\n28-final = (1 - W) * base + W * struct    where the gate holds\n29-final = base                           elsewhere\n30-```\n31-\n32-So the only things \"fitted against the forward PF\" are **W and the gate threshold**. Re-fitting the stage\n33-against a smoothed PF therefore means re-choosing those two — arithmetic over saved arrays, with no PF\n34-re-run. That is why this round splits into a slow producer (row-level PF predictions) and a fast evaluator,\n35-so the re-fit can be iterated without repeating a 3-hour filter run.\n36-\n37:## 2. Reproduction control — and the gate source was not what the aligned frame implied\n38-\n39-Nothing about a modified line is trustworthy until the *unmodified* one can be rebuilt from the same arrays,\n40-so that is step 0 and it gates the rest of the script.\n41-\n42:The first attempt used the aligned frame's own per-row `nnb` / `closest_surv` columns and **did not**\n43:reproduce: 2139 rows wrong (0.057%), max |diff| 4.59 ft, pooled RMSE 8.8634 against the deployed 8.8626.\n44:The residual was concentrated in **6 wells of 760** — `8b95d6d1` alone accounted for 1731 rows. That well\n45:has `closest_surv = 1011.1` in the aligned frame, i.e. just *outside* a `< 1000` gate, yet the deployed line\n46-had gated it **in**.\n47-\n48:The gate is built from `struct_oof.npz`'s **own per-well meta** (`meta_nnb`, `meta_closest`), which differs\n49:from the aligned frame's per-row columns for those boundary wells. With the meta gate:\n50-\n51-```\n52-deployed s_54844628 RMSE                                   8.8626   (ledger: 8.8626)\n53-base (0.5*dwt + 0.5*pf) RMSE                               9.2987   (ledger: 9.2987)\n54-rebuilt (meta gate nnb>=4 & closest<1000, W=0.15) RMSE     8.8626\n55-rows differing by >0.01: 9 of 3,721,471 | max |diff| 0.0182    -> float32 rounding\n56-gated rows: 87.3%                                          (ledger: 87%)\n57-```\n58-\n59-The weight was **recovered from the deployed column itself** rather than taken from the ledger, which is a\n60-stronger check than matching a documented constant:\n61-\n62-```\n63-implied W = (deployed - base) / (struct - base)\n64-  on gated rows : median 0.1500   p5 0.1497   p95 0.1503\n65-  off-gate      : median 0.0000\n66-```\n67-\n68-**The stage is now fully characterised by (meta gate, W = 0.15)**, so substituting a smoothed PF into it is\n69:a clean one-factor change. This also corrects a latent trap for any future round: the aligned frame's\n70:`nnb`/`closest_surv` columns are *not* the deployed gate and silently disagree on 6 wells.\n71-\n72-## 3. What the producer is computing, and why NS=64\n73-\n74-Q56 saved only per-well SSE, so it cannot support a re-fit; the missing artifact is the row-level PF\n75-prediction. The producer emits `pf_fwd` and `pf_sm` per toe row, aligned to `aligned_preds.npz` by\n76-(well, ridx) with the same `ridx`-equality and truth-equality assertions Q36/Q56 use.\n77-\n78-**NS = 64, the deployed configuration.** Q36 and Q56 both ran NS=32 to bound wall-time and both had to carry\n79-a caveat about it. Beyond removing that caveat, NS=64 buys a second control for free: the stored `pf` column\n80-in `aligned_preds.npz` *is* the deployed NS=64 forward PF, so `pf_fwd` can be checked directly against it.\n--\n111-  and if the resulting output is materially different from `54844628` (the gate forbids near-duplicates).\n112-- The **3-well bootstrap 5th was negative at blend level** (−0.3238, P(>0) 0.7919). Q41 showed that\n113-  condition is maximised by changing nothing and Q44 measured the 3-well draw's difference-sd at 2.40 ft, so\n114-  it constrains what a *public* score can resolve rather than novel-well quality. It is a reason to expect a\n115-  noisy public reading, **not** a reason to discount a clean OOF gain.\n116-- Q44 measured slot 2 — the honest line — as the **largest positive term in the final-pair decision**\n117-  (~0.46 ft mean, ~0.94 ft tail, as the decorrelated member of a best-of-2, and the only submission we hold\n118-  whose error is decorrelated from the crowd). Improving it improves the slot the project actually relies\n119-  on. That is the strongest argument for spending one of five idle daily slots, and it will be stated\n120-  explicitly in the submission description if a submission is made.\n121:- Against that: a Kaggle submission would need the smoother reproduced inside the honest kernel. The test\n122-  set is 3 wells / 14,151 rows against the 760 train wells measured here, so runtime is not a constraint.\n123-\n124-## 6. Limits\n125-\n126-- **No end-to-end number yet.** §2 is a control, not a result; §5 is a pre-registration, not a decision.\n127-- The producer runs the PF on **train** wells to build an OOF proxy, as every prior honest-line round has.\n128-- `struct` is reused unchanged from `struct_oof.npz`. Its own construction (IDW over same-group train wells)\n129-  is independent of the PF, so it does not need recomputing — but it was produced once, in July, and is\n130-  taken as given here rather than re-derived.\n131-- The re-fit sweeps W and α. The gate threshold itself (`nnb>=4`, `closest<1000`) is held at the deployed\n7-\n8-## 1. Scope correction, made before running anything\n9-\n10-The task asked for \"Sakoe-Chiba corridor **+ Itakura slope limits**\". **The slope-limit half is already\n11-closed by N1**, and with a strictly better constraint than the generic Itakura cap I would have written.\n12-N1 derived the admissible **per-step** interval from well geometry —\n13-\n14-```\n15-t − s  ∈  [ (−dZ − tan(δmax)·dH)/STEP ,  (−dZ + tan(δmax)·dH)/STEP ]\n16-```\n17-\n18-— and tested `unbounded` / `centred` / `bounded(δmax)` at 4°/8°/16° under nested selection. Verdict:\n19:negative. The band binds 83–97% of transitions, so it is not inert, but it does not improve the DP.\n20-Re-running a generic per-step cap would be a weaker repeat, so it was not done.\n21-\n22-**What was genuinely untested is the CUMULATIVE corridor.** N1's bound is per-step; Sakoe-Chiba's is\n23-global. That distinction is exactly Q40's mechanism — ~477 steps each permitted a fractional move still\n24-permit unbounded *total* drift, so a per-step allowance does not bound accumulation. This round tests only:\n25-\n26-```\n27-admissible states at step j:   |state_j − anchor| ≤ C      (hard mask, not a penalty)\n28-```\n29-\n30-## 2. Two controls, both from prior mistakes\n31-\n32:**Degeneracy control** — `C = ∞` must reproduce the unconstrained `run_dp` exactly:\n33-\n34-```\n35-lam 5    max|corridor(inf) − run_dp| = 0.0000000000\n36-lam 20   ... = 0.0000000000     lam 60 ... = 0.0000000000     lam 150 ... = 0.0000000000\n37-```\n38-\n39:**Binding diagnostic** — N1's first smoke reported its band binding on 0.0% of transitions, which was an\n40-artefact of asking the wrong question and cost a debugging round. Measured directly here:\n41-\n42-```\n43-C=2 → 21.9%    C=3 → 14.7%    C=5 → 5.3%    C=10 → 1.4%    C=20 → 0.4%    C=inf → 0.0%\n44-```\n45-\n46:The first smoke of this task did exactly what N1's did: at `C=40` the corridor bound **0.0%** and changed\n47-nothing. Rather than report that as \"no effect\", the cause was measured — see §3 — and the grid was\n48-re-chosen so the constraint actually bites.\n49-\n50-## 3. The headline — the DP does not wander too far, it wanders far too little\n51-\n52-Deviation of the **unconstrained DP path** from the anchor, against the deviation the **truth** actually\n53-requires (12 wells, grid units ≈ ft):\n54-\n55-```\n56-lam          median        p90        max     TRUTH max\n57-5              11.0       14.0       15.0        19.4\n58-20              2.5        3.0        4.0        19.4\n--\n78-150   13.025   12.991   12.501   12.478   12.478   12.478\n79-```\n80-\n81-The λ=5 row is the informative one: a hard corridor **rescues a badly-tuned low-λ DP by +3.12**\n82-(16.009 → 12.887), landing it right at the level λ alone achieves. So the corridor genuinely does the same\n83-job as the penalty.\n84-\n85-**But it does not do it better.** Best with corridor+λ = **12.138**; best with λ alone = **12.170**. The\n86-two devices are **substitutes**: both suppress movement, and they reach the same place.\n87-\n88-That is a direct answer to Q43's premise. The hypothesis was that a hard cap would do something a soft\n89-penalty structurally cannot — bound accumulation regardless of path length. Measured, they converge to the\n90:same optimum, because in this problem the binding issue is not accumulation.\n91-\n92-## 5. Gate\n93-\n94-```\n95-NESTED (picks [(60, C=10), (150, C=10)])\n96-  selected 12.570  vs unconstrained 12.601  gain +0.031\n97-  helps 2.5% of held-out wells\n98-  3-WELL bootstrap 5th -0.518  50th +0.000  95th +1.528  P(>0) 0.0724\n99-```\n100-\n101-| condition | required | observed | verdict |\n102-|---|---|---|---|\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
